1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,462
Hi, I’m Phillip-Bryan, a junior full-stack developer
pursuing my first professional software engineering role.

2
00:00:06,462 --> 00:00:12,923
Operations Import Desk is an independent learning
demonstration inspired by WebCreek’s public job posting.

3
00:00:12,923 --> 00:00:16,615
It does not represent their systems or clients.

4
00:00:16,615 --> 00:00:24,000
The problem is simple: an operations coordinator receives
shipment CSVs and needs clear accepted and rejected

5
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:26,769
results, even when processing is interrupted.

6
00:00:26,769 --> 00:00:30,000
All the data you’ll see is synthetic.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:35,291
The interface is React, with a NestJS API and Keycloak
sign-in using OIDC.

8
00:00:35,291 --> 00:00:39,360
MongoDB owns the accepted file, business status, attempts
and results.

9
00:00:39,360 --> 00:00:44,651
Redis and BullMQ carry notifications that tell a worker
which import to check.

10
00:00:44,651 --> 00:00:49,535
That distinction matters: losing a queue notification must
not erase accepted work.

11
00:00:49,535 --> 00:00:55,233
I deliberately limited the files to one thousand rows and
two hundred fifty-six kibibytes.

12
00:00:55,233 --> 00:01:00,523
That keeps the result small enough to publish atomically
in one Mongo document.

13
00:01:00,523 --> 00:01:05,000
This is a bounded exercise, not a claim about enterprise
capacity.

14
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:06,485
Here is Submit.

15
00:01:06,485 --> 00:01:11,931
The schema and limits are visible before I choose a file.

16
00:01:11,931 --> 00:01:15,891
I select the valid sample and submit it.

17
00:01:15,891 --> 00:01:23,317
The API confirms that the file was saved; that does not
promise processing has started.

18
00:01:23,317 --> 00:01:28,762
On Details, I can see the actual state, run and attempt.

19
00:01:28,762 --> 00:01:37,178
Small files can finish before the intermediate state is
visible, so there is no artificial progress animation.

20
00:01:37,178 --> 00:01:43,614
Once complete, I get accepted and rejected counts and a
protected CSV download.

21
00:01:43,614 --> 00:01:47,574
I can leave for History, return, or refresh.

22
00:01:47,574 --> 00:01:55,000
The result comes back from the backend rather than
depending on the original browser session.

23
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:59,051
Now I submit the sample with invalid rows.

24
00:01:59,051 --> 00:02:04,114
It contains an impossible date and a duplicated shipment
identifier.

25
00:02:04,114 --> 00:02:09,177
Validation finishes with errors and explains which records
need correction.

26
00:02:09,177 --> 00:02:12,215
Valid rows are still available separately.

27
00:02:12,215 --> 00:02:19,304
Business-data errors are results, not infrastructure
failures, so they do not trigger automatic retries.

28
00:02:19,304 --> 00:02:24,367
The operator corrects the source file and submits changed
bytes.

29
00:02:24,367 --> 00:02:27,405
Downloads go through the authenticated API.

30
00:02:27,405 --> 00:02:35,000
Uploaded values are displayed as text, and spreadsheet
formula prefixes are neutralized when exporting CSVs.

31
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:41,567
For one controlled reliability example, I’m pausing the
local worker process.

32
00:02:41,567 --> 00:02:52,313
This is a real process pause in an isolated demo, not a
simulated status or a database edit.

33
00:02:52,313 --> 00:02:59,478
With the API and Mongo still available, I submit another
valid file.

34
00:02:59,478 --> 00:03:06,642
The file is saved and remains waiting for processing, with
zero attempts.

35
00:03:06,642 --> 00:03:12,612
I leave the page and return to the same import.

36
00:03:12,612 --> 00:03:16,791
Its durable identity and state remain available.

37
00:03:16,791 --> 00:03:26,343
The interface does not diagnose the worker outage; I know
the cause because I paused it.

38
00:03:26,343 --> 00:03:29,925
Now I resume that same worker.

39
00:03:29,925 --> 00:03:33,507
The import completes without another upload.

40
00:03:33,507 --> 00:03:37,687
This demonstrates recovery after a consumer pause.

41
00:03:37,687 --> 00:03:44,851
Separate integration tests cover the harder worker-death
and expired-lease cases, including rejecting

42
00:03:44,851 --> 00:03:47,836
publication from a stale worker.

43
00:03:47,836 --> 00:03:55,000
I’m not claiming this short recording proves all of those
failure modes.

44
00:03:55,000 --> 00:04:01,800
NestJS, Redis, OIDC and queues were learning areas for me
in this project, not prior professional experience.

45
00:04:01,800 --> 00:04:08,600
Codex assisted the design, implementation, tests and
writing, and I need to understand and explain that work.

46
00:04:08,600 --> 00:04:12,600
The handoff includes architecture decisions, evidence and
a recovery runbook.

47
00:04:12,600 --> 00:04:18,600
Docker Compose startup remains unverified, and no hosted
CI result or public application deployment is

48
00:04:18,600 --> 00:04:19,000
claimed.

49
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:27,000
I’d ask a senior engineer to challenge the lease and
fencing model, the OIDC boundaries, and where I may have

50
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:27,400
overengineered.

51
00:04:27,400 --> 00:04:34,200
The goal is a small feature another engineer can review
and maintain, while giving me concrete opportunities

52
00:04:34,200 --> 00:04:35,000
to learn.
